Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: “Company Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, condition, or development that, either individually or in the aggregate, has a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries. However, notwithstanding the foregoing, no event or effect shall be deemed a Material Adverse Effect to the extent that it solely impairs or delays the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated hereby if such impairment or delay arises from any of the following circumstances:
   (i) expected fluctuations in market conditions or economic indices;
   (ii) temporary delays in regulatory approvals or customary third-party consents;
   (iii) variations in the trading characteristics of the Company’s equity that are not accompanied by underlying operational issues.
Clause (xiii) expressly excludes from triggering a Material Adverse Effect any delay or inability to consummate that is attributable solely to the circumstances listed in (i) through (iii). Notwithstanding, if an inability to consummate arises from extraordinary and unforeseen events that are not captured by these exceptions, such inability shall be determinative of a Material Adverse Effect. 


