Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
“Affiliate Material Adverse Effect” means any event, change, or circumstance that, either individually or cumulatively, (a) has a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, or operational results of the Affiliate, or (b) prevents, delays, or impairs the Affiliate’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. However, notwithstanding clause (b), the following events shall not be taken into account when determining whether an Affiliate Material Adverse Effect has occurred with respect to the ability to consummate: 
   (i) changes in general economic conditions, including fluctuations in interest rates, foreign exchange variances, or other commonplace market trends; 
   (ii) modifications in generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP) or their interpretations by relevant authorities; 
   (iii) regulatory shifts that affect the industry broadly without targeting specific operational failures; 
   (iv) impacts arising solely from public announcements or the initial pendency of this Agreement; and 
   (v) market volatility that affects the trading price of the Affiliate’s securities. 
Notwithstanding the exclusions noted above, any event that directly and disproportionately impairs the Affiliate’s ability to consummate the transaction—outside the effects mentioned in clauses (i) through (v)—shall be considered to give rise to an Affiliate Material Adverse Effect.

