Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: 
“Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, or effect that, whether considered individually or in aggregate with other occurrences, (i) has a material adverse impact on the financial condition, business, assets, or operations of the Company or (ii) would prevent, materially delay, or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Provided however, that any of the following occurring after the date hereof shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect unless they result in a delay in consummation extending beyond ninety (90) days: 
 (A) alterations in general economic conditions;
 (B) fluctuations in currency exchange rates;
 (C) changes in the price or trading volume of the Company’s common stock;
 (D) acts of nature, including natural disasters or widespread health emergencies;
 (E) modifications mandated by changes in applicable law or accounting principles;
 (F) actions expressly approved in writing by the Parent. 
Notwithstanding the foregoing, none of the events described in clauses (A), (B), (C), (E), or (F) shall be treated as causing a Material Adverse Effect with respect to the Company’s ability to consummate the Merger, whereas only delays arising under clause (D) that extend beyond the ninety-day threshold may be taken into account for such determination.

