Merger Agreement: 'Substantial Adverse Impact' shall mean any event, change, or development that, either individually or cumulatively, materially impairs (i) the financial condition, operational capability, or long‐term prospects of the Target, or (ii) would reasonably be expected to, in the absence of any limiting exceptions, prevent or materially delay the ability of the Target to consummate the transactions contemplated herein. Notwithstanding the foregoing, this definition expressly excludes as a basis for a Substantial Adverse Impact any effect, change, or development that: (1) arises solely from fluctuations in general market conditions, including without limitation changes in currency exchange rates, interest rates, or broad economic trends; (2) results from modifications in generally applicable accounting standards which do not specifically impair the Target’s core business operations; or (3) is attributable exclusively to known regulatory or legislative adjustments that impact the entire industry rather than the Target in particular. In addition, any impairment in the Target's ability to consummate the transactions that is limited exclusively to delays or uncertainties stemming from factors (1) through (3) shall be expressly excluded from constituting a Substantial Adverse Impact. However, if any such impairment is accompanied by a concurrent, material deterioration in the Target’s underlying business performance not attributable solely to these factors, then such impairment shall be deemed to constitute a Substantial Adverse Impact. 
Question: Based on the provisions of the Agreement, is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
