Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the Agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: “Company Adverse Impact” means any event, change or condition that, either individually or in aggregate with other occurrences, has had or would be reasonably expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company, including its capability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following effects shall be expressly excluded from constituting a Company Adverse Impact:
   (i) any change solely attributable to general market volatility, including fluctuations in the Company’s share price or temporary disruptions in trading; 
   (ii) any delay or impediment to consummation that arises solely from factors routinely accepted by industry practice or from market-related conditions affecting the trading of the Company’s securities;
   (iii) any impairment in the Company’s ability to consummate the transaction that is due exclusively to external market sentiment, provided that such impairment is not accompanied by a breach of key representations or warranties relating to due diligence disclosures.

All effects described in clauses (i) through (iii) are hereby carved out and excluded from the definition of a Company Adverse Impact, even if in combination with other adverse events they might otherwise trigger a Material Adverse Effect determination.
