Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: "Aggregate Material Adverse Effect" shall mean any change, event, circumstance, or development that, individually or in aggregate with other past occurrences, materially adversely affects (i) the financial condition, results of operations, or business prospects of the Acquiring Party and its Subsidiaries, or (ii) impairs, delays, or otherwise hinders the ability of such parties to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Agreement expressly provides that: 
   (a) any adverse effect arising solely as a consequence of general market fluctuations, industry-wide economic slowdowns, or broadly applicable regulatory reforms shall not constitute a Material Adverse Effect, unless such changes disproportionately impact the Acquiring Party relative to comparable industry players; 
   (b) all carveouts enumerated under this Agreement apply exclusively to financial and operational metrics, including changes in GAAP, natural disasters, and other external economic factors; 
   (c) however, no carveout shall be deemed applicable to any adverse impact directly related to the ability to consummate the transaction, which shall remain fully operative as a trigger for a Material Adverse Effect.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to MAE carveouts in the context of this Agreement?
